          "id": 810,
          "type": "multiple-choice",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "band": 3,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Vandana Shiva, a physicist by training who runs seed banks of traditional Indian crop varieties, has long argued that the genetic diversity of cultivated plants is itself a form of agricultural infrastructure. In *Monocultures of the Mind* (1993), she draws together field observations from Indian farms with the global crop-disease record to make a focused claim. Cropping systems built around a few genetically uniform varieties — the high-yield staples that were widely promoted in the late twentieth century — concentrate the entire harvest's risk on a narrow genetic base. When a new pest, an emerging pathogen, or an unusual weather pattern arrives, those uniform fields offer little internal variation to absorb the shock. Diverse traditional cropping systems, by contrast, contain enough genetic variability among their varieties that some plants typically survive what others cannot, and the harvest as a whole is buffered against any single disturbance.",
