          "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The passage tells you that Shiva is making \"a focused claim\" in *Monocultures of the Mind* and then lays that claim out in a clear two-part structure. First, cropping systems built on a few genetically uniform varieties concentrate risk on a narrow genetic base and have little internal variation to absorb the shock of a new pest, pathogen, or unusual weather pattern. Second, diverse traditional cropping systems, by contrast, contain enough genetic variability that some plants typically survive what others cannot, so the harvest as a whole is buffered. Choice A captures exactly this comparison at the right level of generality, names the work, and identifies its evidence base (Indian field observations and the global crop-disease record), which is how the passage frames the argument.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B: scope shift — B picks up a true detail from the opening sentence (Shiva's training as a physicist) and treats it as the main idea. The passage uses that detail only to introduce her; the main idea is the substantive claim *Monocultures of the Mind* advances about genetic uniformity and resilience, not a meta-claim about the analytical flavor of her work.\n- Choice C: surface match — C echoes the passage's phrase about \"the high-yield staples that were widely promoted in the late twentieth century\" but extends Shiva's argument into a sweeping empirical claim — that those varieties have \"failed in nearly every Indian region\" — that the passage never makes. The passage says uniform fields have less internal variation to absorb shocks, not that they have collapsed everywhere.\n- Choice D: inverse — D inverts the central comparison. The passage's whole point is that diverse traditional systems are buffered against any single disturbance because their genetic variability lets some plants survive what others cannot; D has them suffering \"comparably\" to uniform systems under severe weather, which contradicts the contrast the passage is built on."
