          "correctAnswer": "A",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The passage presents two parallel observations and asks what condition they jointly license. At hydrothermal vents, tube worms host sulfur-oxidizing bacteria and obtain nutrition from the bacteria's chemical byproducts; at cold seeps, deep-sea mussels host methane-oxidizing bacteria in their gills and thrive far below the photic zone. The two cases share two structural features: (1) a non-sunlight chemical energy source is present at the habitat (sulfur compounds at vents, methane at seeps), and (2) the resident animals carry symbiotic bacteria capable of converting that chemical energy into nutrition the host can use. Choice A states exactly this minimum joint condition. Both halves of the conjunction are required by the passage, and neither is stronger than what the passage establishes.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A is correct; the others fail in characteristic ways.\n- Choice B: scope shift — B narrows the inference to a specific cross-host claim: that sulfur-oxidizing bacteria from tube worms can also be cultivated in mussel gills. The passage establishes a structural parallel between two distinct symbioses (sulfur with tube worms, methane with mussels); it does not claim that the two bacterial partners are interchangeable across hosts, and the licensed inference does not depend on that further claim.\n- Choice C: inverse — C reintroduces sunlight-driven primary production through the back door, suggesting that occasional organic fallout from the photic zone is what makes the communities viable. The passage's whole point in pairing the two studies is that vents and seeps support dense animal life *without* depending on photosynthetic input; C reverses that finding.\n- Choice D: surface match — D borrows the right vocabulary (animals, bacteria, chemical compounds) but inverts the dependency relationship. The passage describes a symbiosis in which the host obtains nutrition *through* the bacterial partner; D imagines the host surviving *without* the bacterial partner as long as the chemicals remain in the water. That severs the very mechanism the studies establish."
