          "correctAnswer": "A",
          "explanation": "The passage gives two specific premises about Doudna's stance: she continues to advocate for research on somatic therapies aimed at otherwise untreatable diseases, and she has publicly endorsed a moratorium on the clinical use of germline editing until broader societies can deliberate. Together these establish exactly one minimum inference about what a developer of a powerful editing tool may do: she may press for restraint on a particular set of clinical uses (here, germline editing in human embryos) while still pursuing research on a different set (here, somatic therapies). Choice A states that licensed inference at the scope the premises actually warrant.\n\nAt this difficulty level, the key test is sufficient-vs-necessary. The premises license the claim that scientists may combine targeted restraint with continued research; they do not license stronger claims about ultimate authority, categorical condemnation, or universal prohibition.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B: surface match — the passage talks about scientists who help develop a powerful editing tool, and this option recycles that framing. But endorsing a moratorium until societies can deliberate is the opposite of claiming ultimate decision-making authority. Doudna asks for collective deliberation, not for scientists to be the deciders. The premises license a claim about advocacy, not about who bears final responsibility.\n- Choice C: scope shift — this option pushes Doudna's position from a conditional moratorium (a pause until societies deliberate) to a categorical ethical condemnation (heritable modification \"indefensible regardless of medical benefit\"). The passage explicitly frames the moratorium as a hold pending deliberation, not as a permanent ban on principle. The premises support a measured, conditional claim, and this option overshoots into an absolute one.\n- Choice D: inverse — this option contradicts a stated premise. Doudna continues to advocate for somatic therapies in human cells, which is a clinical use of the tool in human cells. To conclude that the tool is too dangerous to use in human cells of any kind is to deny precisely the part of her position the passage spells out."
