          "explanation": "The claim is narrow and specific: the satellite record demonstrates that the rate of global mean sea-level rise has been accelerating across the past three decades, rather than holding constant. Supporting that claim requires evidence that the rate in a later decadal window is meaningfully higher than the rate in an earlier one, with the trend tracking upward across the intermediate decades. Choice A meets that demand precisely. It contrasts the latest decade (2013-2022 at 4.5 mm per year) with the earliest (1993-2002 at 2.7 mm per year), showing a substantial increase, and it also notes that intermediate windows generally lie between the two extremes — which is what an upward trend should look like in a noisy record.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B is a surface match of the kind hard COE-Q items reliably trap. The 102 mm cumulative figure is the largest absolute number that can be reported from the satellite record, and it is genuinely true. But the claim is not that sea level has risen by a great deal; the claim is that the rate has accelerated. A single cumulative-rise figure says nothing about whether the rate was constant or changing across the decades.\n- Choice C uses a real comparison from the table but reads the data in the wrong direction relative to the claim. The 1998-2007 rate (3.1 mm per year) is higher than the 2003-2012 rate (3.0 mm per year), so the cited values actually reflect a tiny dip between two adjacent windows, not an acceleration. Pairing data showing a small drop with a conclusion of acceleration is the inverse pattern the rubric warns against.\n- Choice D is true at the level of arithmetic — the 3.8 mm-per-year rate in 2008-2017 does fall between the rates of the earliest and latest windows — but the conclusion drawn from that fact contradicts the claim under investigation. \"Essentially steady\" is the opposite of accelerating, so this option supports a competing claim, not the student's.",
