          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "command-of-evidence-textual",
          "passage": "Mercedes Pascual studies how climate variability shapes the dynamics of infectious disease, and her work has focused especially on cholera in Bangladesh, where outbreaks have recurred for more than a century. Earlier accounts of those recurrent epidemics emphasized population immunity: as a generation of survivors aged out of the population, susceptibility was thought to recover, allowing a new wave of disease. Pascual and her collaborators have proposed an additional driver. They argue that the timing of the multi-year cycles in cholera incidence in coastal Bangladesh is statistically paced by the El Niño-Southern Oscillation (ENSO), with shifts in sea-surface temperature in the Pacific propagating, by way of regional climate, into the conditions that allow cholera to spread. *Pascual's hypothesis is that cholera cycles in coastal Bangladesh follow ENSO-driven climate signals rather than being explained by population-immunity dynamics alone.*",
          "question": "Which finding from Pascual's research, if true, would most directly support the hypothesis?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "id": "A",
              "text": "In a long time series of cholera cases from coastal Bangladesh, the statistical association between cholera incidence and ENSO indices recorded several months earlier was stronger than the association between cholera incidence and modeled estimates of waning population immunity over the same period."
            },
            {
              "id": "B",
              "text": "A study of coastal villages found that cholera transmission rose sharply during periods of unusually warm sea-surface temperatures in the nearby Bay of Bengal."
            },
            {
              "id": "C",
              "text": "Outbreaks in two interior districts of Bangladesh, far from the coast, were correlated with seasonal monsoon rainfall but showed no measurable association with ENSO indices."
            },
            {
              "id": "D",
              "text": "A reanalysis of long-term cholera records found that ENSO indices accounted for essentially none of the interannual variation in cholera incidence in coastal Bangladesh, while reconstructed estimates of waning immunity accounted for nearly all of it."
            }
          ],
          "correctAnswer": "A",
          "explanation": "Pascual's hypothesis is comparative: she is not merely arguing that climate matters for cholera in coastal Bangladesh, but that climate signals (specifically ENSO) explain the multi-year cycles better than population-immunity dynamics alone. Supporting that hypothesis directly therefore requires a finding that pits the two candidate drivers against each other and shows ENSO winning. Choice A does exactly that. It reports a long time series from coastal Bangladesh, places ENSO indices and immunity dynamics side by side as competing predictors, and finds that ENSO is the stronger statistical predictor. The match between the finding and the hypothesis is direct, the scale (coastal Bangladesh, multi-year time series) is right, and the head-to-head structure mirrors the claim Pascual is making.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B is a surface match. Warm sea-surface temperatures and coastal villages echo the language of Pascual's framing, and a sharp rise in cholera transmission during warm-SST periods sounds like exactly the kind of finding that ought to support a climate-driven story. But the hypothesis is not that warm SSTs alone correlate with outbreaks; it is that ENSO-paced climate signals explain the cycles better than immunity dynamics. A SST-cholera correlation does not engage the immunity comparison the claim depends on.\n- Choice C is a scope shift. The hypothesis is specifically about coastal Bangladesh, where ENSO signals are expected to propagate via sea-surface temperature into local conditions; interior districts are a different setting with different climate inputs. A null ENSO finding inland tells you about the geographic limits of the climate signal, not about whether it paces the coastal cycles the hypothesis is concerned with.\n- Choice D is an inverse. The hypothesis is that ENSO outperforms immunity dynamics as an explanation; this finding reports the opposite, with immunity accounting for nearly all the variance and ENSO essentially none. Choosing D would substitute a finding that weakens Pascual's claim for one that supports it."
