          "correctAnswer": "C",
          "explanation": "The blank sits between two complete independent clauses. The first, \"The codex was no minor curiosity,\" has its own subject (\"The codex\") and verb (\"was\") and could stand alone as a sentence. The second, \"its painted pages recorded the genealogies and political alliances of pre-Columbian noble lineages and became a central primary source for the historical study of the Mixtec,\" likewise has its own subject (\"its painted pages\") and finite verbs (\"recorded\" and \"became\") and could also stand alone. When two independent clauses are joined without a coordinating conjunction (and, but, or, nor, for, so, yet), Standard English requires a semicolon, a period, or a colon. Choice C supplies the conventional semicolon, which signals the close logical relationship between the two clauses without forcing a conjunction.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A (\"curiosity,\"): a comma alone between two independent clauses produces a comma splice. The option resembles the correct comma-plus-coordinating-conjunction pattern (e.g., \"curiosity, and\"), but with the conjunction stripped away the comma is no longer doing enough work to hold the clauses together.\n- Choice B (\"curiosity\"): omitting punctuation entirely creates a run-on sentence. Two complete clauses cannot simply be jammed together with no boundary mark; some terminal mark, semicolon, or comma-plus-conjunction is required.\n- Choice D (\"curiosity, and\"): the comma plus coordinating conjunction is mechanically grammatical, but the relation between the two clauses is one of explanation (the second tells us why the codex was no minor curiosity), not parallel addition. \"And\" treats the second clause as one more item in a list rather than as the substantiation of the first; the test here is the standard clause-boundary punctuation test, and only the semicolon supplies the canonical mark for joining two independent clauses without a conjunction."
