          "explanation": "The subject of the verb is the plural noun phrase \"strontium isotope ratios,\" which requires the plural verb \"indicate.\" The intervening prepositional phrases \"in the teeth of individuals buried just outside the city\" contain several singular nouns (\"city,\" \"fraction\"), but a chain of prepositional phrases placed between a subject and its verb does not change the number of the subject. A plural subject takes a plural verb regardless of intervening singular modifiers, so \"ratios . . . indicate\" is the correct construction. Choice C supplies the simple-present plural form, which also matches the surrounding present-tense expository voice (\"examines,\" \"reveal\").\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A (\"indicates\"): surface match — this is the singular form of the present-tense verb, and a student might pick it because it visually agrees with the closer singular noun \"city\" or \"fraction.\" That is the classic proximity-agreement error: the verb is being made to agree with the nearest noun rather than with the actual head of the subject phrase, \"ratios.\"\n- Choice B (\"is indicating\"): scope shift — this option is also singular (\"is\" is the singular auxiliary) and additionally swaps the simple present for the present progressive. It commits both an agreement error and an aspect mismatch, since the surrounding sentence states a stable scientific finding rather than an action in progress.\n- Choice D (\"has indicated\"): inverse of the right number — \"has\" is the singular auxiliary form of \"have,\" so this choice agrees with a singular subject rather than the plural \"ratios\"; the plural counterpart would be \"have indicated.\" Even with the number error fixed, present perfect is a softer match than the simple present that the rest of the passage uses, but the disqualifying problem is the number mismatch."
