          "explanation": "The blank sits between two complete independent clauses. The first clause is \"these objects were not passive markers of European trade,\" with subject \"these objects\" and verb \"were\"; the second clause is \"they were locally reinterpreted as ritual and political instruments by the West African societies that received them,\" with subject \"they\" and verb \"were reinterpreted.\" The relationship between the two clauses is explanatory: the second clause spells out what the first clause's negation is asserting. Standard English treats a colon as the conventional mark for joining two independent clauses where the second elaborates on or specifies the first, so choice B (\"were not passive markers of European trade:\") is correct. A period or semicolon would also be grammatically possible boundaries, but among the four choices given, only the colon supplies a valid boundary mark.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A (\"were not passive markers of European trade,\"): surface match — a comma alone between two independent clauses produces a comma splice. The option resembles the correct comma-plus-coordinating-conjunction pattern, but with no coordinating conjunction (\"and,\" \"but,\" etc.) following it, the comma cannot, by itself, hold two independent clauses together.\n- Choice C (\"were not passive markers of European trade\"): scope shift — omitting punctuation entirely fuses the two independent clauses into a run-on. Two independent clauses cannot simply abut with no boundary mark at all; some terminal-strength punctuation (period, semicolon, colon, or comma + conjunction) is required.\n- Choice D (\"were not passive markers of European trade, however\"): inverse — \"however\" is a conjunctive adverb, not a coordinating conjunction, so it cannot be preceded by a comma to legally join two independent clauses. Even setting that aside, \"however\" signals contrast, but the second clause does not contrast with the first; it specifies what the first clause means. The connector pulls the logical relation in the opposite direction from the elaboration the sentence is doing."
