          "correctAnswer": "B",
          "explanation": "The sentence presents a coordinated list of three nouns naming life events: \"births, accessions, and ______ of individual rulers.\" Standard English requires the items in a coordinated list to be parallel in form. \"Births\" and \"accessions\" are bare plural nouns introduced by no determiner of their own, so the third item must also be a bare plural noun. \"Deaths\" supplies that form exactly, completing the parallel triplet (births, accessions, deaths) and continuing to take \"of individual rulers\" as its postmodifier. Choice B is therefore correct.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A (\"the deaths\"): surface match — \"the deaths\" is a real noun phrase, and a student might add \"the\" because the noun seems to want a determiner. But the first two items in the list (\"births,\" \"accessions\") are themselves bare plurals with no \"the,\" so inserting \"the\" only on the third item breaks parallel structure. Standard English treats coordinated items as taking the same level of marking; the determiner must either be present on all three items or on none.\n- Choice C (\"dying\"): scope shift — \"dying\" is a gerund / participial form, not a plural noun, so it does not match the bare-plural form of the first two items. Even though \"dying\" can sometimes function as a noun (\"the dying of light\"), here it would force the reader to parse \"births, accessions, and dying of individual rulers\" as mixing two grammatical categories: count nouns followed by a process noun. CB treats this as a failure of parallel form.\n- Choice D (\"they died\"): inverse — this option swaps a noun for a finite clause (subject + verb in past tense). The list demands a noun, so inserting an independent-clause-style fragment yields \"births, accessions, and they died of individual rulers,\" which is not English. The category mismatch is the most extreme of the three distractors and is the inverse of the parallel-noun requirement."
