          "explanation": "The blank is the subject of the verb \"found\" in the main clause, and its referent is established in the preceding subordinate clause: \"the astronomer Vera Rubin and her collaborators.\" That noun phrase is plural — Rubin plus her collaborators — so Standard English requires a plural pronoun. \"They\" is the third-person plural subject pronoun and supplies both the correct number and the correct case (subject of \"found\"). Choice B is therefore correct.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A (\"she\"): inverse — \"she\" is the singular feminine subject pronoun and would refer to Rubin alone, not to Rubin and her collaborators jointly. The opening clause makes the joint subject explicit by coordinating \"Rubin\" with \"her collaborators,\" so a singular pronoun in the main clause drops the collaborators from the sentence entirely. The pronoun number disagrees with the antecedent.\n- Choice C (\"it\"): scope shift — \"it\" is a singular pronoun whose plausible antecedents in the surrounding text would be \"the survey\" or \"the result.\" Substituting \"it\" yields \"when the astronomer Vera Rubin and her collaborators completed their 1980 survey . . . , it found . . . ,\" which makes the survey itself the agent of finding, an unintended and inanimate-subject reading. The pronoun fails to agree with the named human subject of the action.\n- Choice D (\"one\"): surface match — \"one\" can serve as an indefinite pronoun in formal writing, and a student might pick it because it sounds appropriately academic. But \"one\" is generic (\"a person in general\") rather than referring back to a specific antecedent, so it severs the connection between the main-clause subject and the joint subject just named in the subordinate clause. Pronouns in this construction must agree with and refer to the named antecedent, not introduce a new generic referent."
