          "correctAnswer": "A",
          "explanation": "The first sentences establish a hostile reception: Eddington publicly dismissed Chandrasekhar's 1931 calculation, and his standing was such that the upper mass limit was treated as an open question for years. The closing sentence reports that observations over the following decades steadily confirmed the limit Chandrasekhar had derived. The logical relation is concession-and-confirmation: in spite of the senior astronomer's dismissal, the result was eventually vindicated by independent astronomical evidence. \"Nevertheless,\" is the CB-lexicon transition for that \"in defiance of the headwind just described, the following turned out to be true\" move, and is correct (A). The key word that selects \"Nevertheless,\" rather than the more generic \"However,\" is \"steadily.\" The closing sentence is not placing Eddington's view side-by-side with Chandrasekhar's as a parallel A-vs-B contrast; it is reporting that the result Eddington tried to push aside survived and accumulated evidence in its favor over time, which is the precise relation \"Nevertheless,\" is built for.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B: \"Therefore,\" is the inverse of the actual logical relation. It signals that the closing sentence is a causal consequence of what came before, but here the later confirmations did not follow as a result of Eddington's dismissal; they vindicated the calculation in spite of it. A reader who chooses \"Therefore,\" reverses the cause-and-effect direction the passage actually establishes.\n- Choice C: \"Likewise,\" signals that the closing sentence presents a parallel case along the same line of thought as the previous sentences. The relation here is not parallel: the prior sentences describe a public rejection of a result, the closing sentence describes that result being confirmed by later observations. \"Likewise,\" would fit if both halves were examples of dismissals or both were confirmations; neither holds.\n- Choice D: \"Moreover,\" signals that the closing sentence adds another point of the same kind onto the first, building cumulatively. The surface impression that all sentences are about the reception of Chandrasekhar's calculation can make this feel like a smooth continuation, but the closing sentence is not adding another reason to think the result was rejected; it is reporting that the rejection was overcome. \"Moreover,\" treats the relation as additive when it is actually concessive, and so points in the wrong direction."
