          "explanation": "The correct answer is A, \"resisted.\" The passage states that for decades after McClintock's 1950 paper, the wider field of genetics did not accept her claim that genetic elements could move within the genome, and only after the molecular techniques of the 1970s caught up did mainstream geneticists come to agree. The blank therefore needs a verb whose object is McClintock's conclusion and whose meaning is \"refused to accept\" or \"pushed back against.\" \"Resisted\" fits exactly: to resist a conclusion is to refuse to accept it without necessarily refuting it, which is precisely what the closing clause describes (the wider field accepted the conclusion only later, once the technology caught up).\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B: scope shift — \"underestimated\" has the right general direction (a negative response to McClintock's claim) but the wrong specificity. To underestimate a conclusion is to grant that it has some merit but to assign it less weight than it deserves; what the passage describes is a stronger response, in which mainstream geneticists held back from agreeing that the genome was anything but a fixed sequence of stable loci until decades later. The sentence calls for refusal-to-accept, not low estimation.\n- Choice C: surface match — \"embellished\" sounds at home in academic prose about a contested claim (\"critics embellished the original finding\") and pairs grammatically with \"her conclusion,\" so the word can feel natural in the slot. But to embellish a conclusion is to add ornamental detail to it, which has nothing to do with the field's actual response. Mainstream geneticists were not dressing McClintock's conclusion up; they were declining to accept it. The collocation pulls students toward a word whose denotation the sentence does not support.\n- Choice D: inverse — \"advocated\" is the near-opposite of what the passage describes. To advocate a conclusion is to argue publicly in support of it, but the closing clause makes clear that the wider field did not come around to McClintock's conclusion until much later. Choosing \"advocated\" would mean the geneticists who later accepted her work had been arguing for it all along, reversing the cause-and-effect relationship the passage spells out."
