          "skill": "words-in-context",
          "passage": "Curator and art historian Hetti Perkins of the Australian National University argues in *Art + Soul: A Journey into the World of Aboriginal Art* (2010) that the popular reception of Aboriginal Australian painting as decorative regional ornament adapted to Western canvas seriously misreads what is happening on the painted surface. The works she discusses, she contends, are best understood as a continuing system through which knowledge of Country—its sites, songs, kinship obligations, and legal protocols—is transmitted across generations of painters and viewers within the producing communities. A senior painter who lays out a particular ground design, on this account, is not selecting an attractive pattern but invoking specific obligations to a place, and a junior painter who is permitted to paint that design has been entrusted with the right to do so. To approach the paintings as ornaments rather than as records of cosmological and legal knowledge, Perkins suggests, is to remain ____ the framework that gives them their meaning in those communities.",
