          "skill": "text-structure-and-purpose",
          "passage": "In her 1988 essay collection *The Inspired Dream: Life as Art in Aboriginal Australia*, the curator Margie West, of the Museum and Art Gallery of the Northern Territory, examined the bark paintings produced by Yolngu and other Arnhem Land artists. Earlier museum catalogues had often described such works as decorative records of an artist's surroundings, sometimes prized for their craftsmanship and sometimes admired for their imagery, but in either case treated as objects whose meaning was largely on the surface. West argued that the same painting can carry a meaning that is openly accessible to any viewer and another, deeper meaning that only initiated members of the artist's community are entitled to read. A senior member of the community, on her account, sees the bark not merely as a painting but as a restricted document whose details can be revealed, withheld, or selectively shared in ways the gallery viewer is unlikely to detect.",
