              "label": "Text 2",
              "text": "The Renaissance scholar Catherine Belsey, in The Subject of Tragedy (1985), works within much the same historicist commitment as Greenblatt: the plays of the period are best read against the cultural and political grammar of early modern England, and they participate in shaping the subject that grammar produces. Where she parts company is on how cleanly that subject is held in place. Belsey reads the period drama, Shakespeare's included, as staging a recurring crisis in the very category of the unified speaking self — particularly in the figures of women, servants, and others to whom early modern discourse offered no stable position from which to speak. The plays, on her account, register fissures in early modern subjectivity that the closing acts cannot smoothly resolve: a daughter's refusal, a wife's silence, a player's metatheatrical aside, all leave openings the period's dominant vocabulary could not fully suture. Containment, in Belsey's view, is therefore one possible outcome of staged subversion, but it is not the master pattern; the plays equally often leave their fissures uncovered, available to be read by audiences then and now as evidence that the early modern self was less settled than the period's official self-descriptions implied."
