          "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Text 2 is explicit about the methodological ground Belsey shares with Greenblatt: the plays \"are best read against the cultural and political grammar of early modern England,\" and they participate in shaping the subject that grammar produces. Her quarrel is not with the historicist starting point but with how cleanly it is supposed to deliver containment. She reads the period drama as staging a \"recurring crisis in the very category of the unified speaking self,\" particularly in figures — women, servants, others — for whom early modern discourse offered no stable position. Those moments, on her account, leave fissures the closing acts cannot smoothly suture: a daughter's refusal, a wife's silence, a player's metatheatrical aside. Her closing claim is the load-bearing one: containment is \"one possible outcome,\" but \"not the master pattern,\" and the plays often leave their fissures uncovered. Choice B captures exactly that combination — historicism preserved, closure logic rejected, fissures (with the same exemplars Belsey names) presented as evidence the closing acts do not smoothly resolve.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A: surface match — A casts Belsey as repudiating historicism altogether and reading Shakespeare as autonomous aesthetic objects. Text 2 says the opposite in its first sentence: she works within \"much the same historicist commitment\" as Greenblatt. A confuses her resistance to one of historicism's typical conclusions (containment) with a refusal of the methodological frame they actually share.\n- Choice C: inverse — C reverses Belsey's stance by treating containment as correct for the major tragedies and explaining away counterexamples as later collaborators' work. Text 2 explicitly denies that containment is the master pattern and grounds the counterexamples (resisting daughter, silent wife, metatheatrical aside) in the plays themselves, not in posthumous interventions. The choice flips Belsey from a critic of containment into one of its defenders.\n- Choice D: scope shift — D imports a methodological controversy (neuroscientific accounts of audience response) that has no anchor in either text. Belsey's disagreement with Greenblatt is internal to historicist literary scholarship; she contests the closure logic from within shared commitments, not from a different discipline. The scope shift turns a precise intramural quarrel into a cross-disciplinary supersession."
