          "band": 3,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "In her 2018 book Curatorial Activism: Towards an Ethics of Curating, the curator and scholar Maura Reilly compiles decades of attendance, acquisition, and exhibition data from major museums and international biennials and asks why the demographic composition of collections and group shows has shifted so little since the feminist critique of museum practice took shape in the 1970s. Reilly's main argument is that the modest changes that have occurred in those numbers cannot be attributed to a slow loosening of art-world taste. Where she finds measurable improvement — in the proportion of women artists, artists of color, and artists from underrepresented regions — it tracks the work of specific curators who set explicit programmatic goals and built exhibitions, biennials, and acquisition agendas around them. Where curators left openness to the field's gradual drift, by contrast, the demographic mix barely moved. Reilly therefore argues that representation in major institutions is a function of deliberate curatorial intervention rather than of any passive cultural diffusion, and she proposes the practice of \"curatorial activism\" as the remedy.",
