          "band": 3,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "In Whitewalling: Art, Race, and Protest in 3 Acts (2018), the critic Aruna D'Souza examines three episodes from American museum history in which works on display were challenged by Black artists, scholars, and community members on grounds tied to the depiction of Black suffering. In each case — a 1969 Metropolitan Museum exhibition on Harlem, a 1979 Artists Space show, and a painting included in the 2017 Whitney Biennial — the institution responded with a similar appeal to artistic freedom and curatorial neutrality, treating the protests as pressure to be weathered rather than as substantive critiques to be answered. D'Souza is interested less in the merits of any one work than in the recurring institutional pattern. The same script, she shows, is rehearsed across decades and across very different museums: the curated artwork is defended, the protest is framed as an external constraint on free expression, and the perspective of the depicted community is treated as one input to be balanced. Institutional commitments to neutrality, on D'Souza's reading, routinely operate to override those protests rather than to engage them.",
