          "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The passage organizes itself around a recurring institutional pattern. The opening sentence introduces three episodes from American museum history in which works depicting Black suffering were challenged by Black artists, scholars, and community members. The second sentence describes the institutional response common to all three: an appeal to artistic freedom and curatorial neutrality, with the protests treated as pressure to weather rather than as substantive critiques to engage. The next sentences sharpen the claim — D'Souza is interested less in any one work than in the recurring institutional pattern, and the same script is rehearsed across decades and across very different museums. The closing sentence draws the load-bearing conclusion: institutional commitments to neutrality routinely operate to override those protests rather than to engage them. Choice A captures exactly that thesis, naming the three-episode scope, the institutional appeal, and the override-rather-than-engage characterization the passage settles on.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B: scope shift — B treats the 1969 Harlem-themed Metropolitan exhibition as the central case and demotes the other two episodes to background timing. The passage instead positions all three episodes as parallel instances of the same pattern and explicitly says D'Souza is interested in the recurring institutional response rather than in any one work. Promoting one of the three to primary status narrows a pattern claim into a case study.\n- Choice C: inverse — C reverses the pattern the passage describes. The text's whole point is that institutions respond to these protests by appealing to neutrality and artistic freedom, treating community perspective as one input to be balanced — that is, by overriding rather than revising. C states a story of consistent policy revision in response to protest, which is the opposite of what D'Souza is arguing.\n- Choice D: surface match — the passage does refer to artistic freedom and to a span of museum history from 1969 through 2017, so a hurried reader can mistake that surface content for a story about how artistic-freedom norms developed in curatorial practice. But the text never describes any expansion of legal definitions or any historical development of the concept; the artistic-freedom appeal is offered as a recurring institutional response D'Souza wants to characterize, not as a doctrine whose history she is tracking."
