          "correctAnswer": "B",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The passage uses a headline-then-refinement structure typical of hard central-ideas items. The first two sentences set up the apparent claim that early readers fastened on: a catalogue of inverted gender categories in particular New Guinea societies. The third sentence pivots with \"however,\" signaling that this headline reading is not Mead's principal argument. The fourth and fifth sentences then state the deeper claim Mead actually developed: cross-cultural variation in how temperaments are mapped onto male and female bodies is so wide that those mappings cannot be a simple expression of biology, so the temperamental contents of masculinity and femininity are largely cultural. Choice B captures both layers of the structure — the popular reception and the underlying argument — and identifies the deeper argument as the main idea at the right level of generalization.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A: surface match — A restates the headline observation that opens the passage, which is exactly the framing the passage flags as incomplete. The detail about inverted temperaments is real and accurate, but the third sentence explicitly tells the reader that Mead's argument was \"not principally about\" which society had inverted the categories. Treating the inversion catalogue as the main idea mistakes the setup for the thesis the passage spends its second half developing.\n- Choice C: inverse — C reverses the conclusion of Mead's argument. The passage closes by saying that the cross-cultural distributions could not be the simple expression of biology and that the temperamental contents of masculinity and femininity are largely a cultural construction. C claims the opposite, that the New Guinea data \"confirmed\" a reliable correspondence between temperament and male and female bodies in every society. That switch from \"largely cultural\" to a reliable biological correspondence flips the central claim.\n- Choice D: scope shift — D shifts the focus from Mead's argument to the sociology of her reception, treating reviewers' unfamiliarity with Columbia-style cultural anthropology as the main point. The passage does discuss how reviewers read the book, but only as the foil that motivates the refinement. Substituting an explanation for the misreading in place of the argument the passage actually develops collapses the main idea into a side issue."
