          "id": 838,
          "type": "multiple-choice",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "band": 3,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "Trained in anthropology at Barnard, Zora Neale Hurston returned in the late 1920s to the Black communities of central and northern Florida to record folktales, work songs, and conjure practices for what would become *Mules and Men* (1935). Earlier collectors had typically printed such material as bare texts, treating each tale as a fixed cultural artifact whose meaning could be read off its words alone. Hurston, by contrast, devoted page after page to the porches, jukes, and turpentine camps where the tales were told, to the back-and-forth between teller and audience, and to the small jokes and challenges that shaped how a story was received in the moment. Her presentation suggests that scholars hoping to grasp what these tales actually meant to the people who told them should ____",
