          "difficulty": "hard",
          "band": 4,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "Writing as both a trained linguist and a member of the Yankton Sioux community whose life she was describing, Ella Cara Deloria argued in *Speaking of Indians* (1944) that Sioux society was organized through an extensive web of kinship obligations whose categories — relations among siblings, cousins of various degrees, in-laws, and ceremonial kin — assigned specific responsibilities for care, naming, gift-giving, and instruction. Many of these categories had no equivalent in the bounded nuclear-family unit that mid-century United States policymakers treated as the standard household for purposes of allotment, enrollment, and welfare administration. Deloria did not dispute that Sioux households existed or that they raised children; her point was that the practical work of raising a Sioux child was distributed across the kinship web rather than confined to the parents alone. A reader of Deloria therefore has reason to expect that a federal policy that recorded only the nuclear-family unit when documenting a Sioux household would ____",
