          "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The passage builds two premises. First, Deloria argued that Sioux society was organized through an extensive web of kinship obligations whose categories assigned specific responsibilities for care, naming, gift-giving, and instruction, and that many of those categories had no equivalent in the nuclear-family unit federal administrators used. Second, Deloria did not dispute that Sioux households existed or raised children; her point was that the practical work of raising a Sioux child was distributed across the kinship web rather than confined to the parents alone. The minimum inference those premises force is that a policy recording only the nuclear-family unit would fail to capture obligations Deloria locates outside the parent-child unit. Choice A states exactly that scoped consequence. At this difficulty, the test is sufficient-vs-necessary: the premises license a claim about what the policy would miss, not about whether it succeeds in other goals or requires linguistic training to administer.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B: inverse — B reverses the second premise. The passage states that the practical work of raising a child was distributed across the kinship web rather than confined to the parents alone; B claims Deloria conceded that Sioux households operated essentially like the nuclear-family units federal administrators assumed. That switch from \"distributed across\" to \"essentially like\" flips the position Deloria develops.\n- Choice C: scope shift — this is the load-bearing trap. C is consistent with the passage's emphasis on kinship beyond the nuclear unit, but it overshoots what the premises license. Deloria explicitly does not dispute that Sioux households existed or raised children, so children had identifiable parents; her claim is that obligations beyond those parents were missed by nuclear-family bookkeeping, not that children belonged only to ceremonial kin and would be invisible to a household-based record.\n- Choice D: surface match — D borrows Deloria's status as a linguist and the mention of Dakota and Lakota languages to construct a procedural condition for usable records. The passage does not connect linguistic training to administrative usability; it connects the inadequacy of nuclear-family categories to a kinship system whose obligations exceed them. Inferring that policymakers themselves must become linguists turns a structural argument into an unrelated procedural requirement."
