          "type": "multiple-choice",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "band": 3,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "command-of-evidence-textual",
          "passage": "Thomas Hardy's 1874 novel *Far from the Madding Crowd* opens with an extended portrait of the shepherd Gabriel Oak. The opening alternates between two registers, sketching Oak as he appears on Sundays — when his stiff best clothes and his self-conscious churchgoing manner produce a slightly comic, hesitant figure — and as he appears during the long ordinary span of the working week, when his bearing is more settled and unhurried. A literary critic, examining how Hardy chooses to organize this two-sided portrait, argues that Hardy ultimately locates Oak's character less in any one striking feature than in the steady working-week demeanor, and that the narrator of the novel presents that everyday bearing, rather than the awkward Sunday self or any moment of dramatic emotion, as the truest available index of who Oak really is.",
