          "correctAnswer": "B",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is B, \"stance:\". The blank sits between a complete independent clause — \"Anthropologist Audra Simpson, who teaches at Columbia University, treats the steady refusal by the Mohawks of Kahnawake to accept Canadian or U.S. citizenship as a substantive political stance\" — and a second independent clause that explains and elaborates on the meaning of that stance: \"on her account, what looks from the outside like a simple absence is in fact an ongoing exercise of Mohawk authority that cannot be reduced to a gap left by political withdrawal.\" Standard English uses a colon to attach an elaborating or explanatory clause to a preceding complete sentence — the relation here is precisely that, with the second clause unpacking what makes Simpson's framing of refusal substantive. Choice B supplies the colon and lets the elaboration land cleanly.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A (\"stance,\"): surface match — placing a comma between two independent clauses produces a comma splice. The boundary looks like an ordinary punctuation pause, but a comma alone cannot, in Standard English, hold two independent clauses together; some stronger mark — a colon, a semicolon, a period, or a comma plus a coordinating conjunction — is required.\n- Choice C (\"stance\"): inverse — supplying no punctuation at all is the opposite of the correct treatment. With nothing between \"stance\" and \"on her account,\" the two independent clauses are jammed into a single fused (run-on) sentence, leaving the reader no signal that a clause boundary has been reached.\n- Choice D (\"stance, and\"): scope shift — a different SEC error. A comma plus a coordinating conjunction is mechanically grammatical between two independent clauses, but \"and\" misrepresents the relation between them: the second clause is not an additional, parallel point but an elaboration that explains the substantive content of the stance just named. A coordinating conjunction loses the explanatory force a colon supplies, and the test here is which mark fits the elaborating relation, not whether any clause-joining mark is grammatical."
