          "correctAnswer": "B",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is B, \"offers.\" The grammatical subject of the verb is the singular noun phrase \"the slow response,\" not the plural noun \"organizations\" embedded in the prepositional phrase \"of mainstream Black political organizations.\" The parenthetical \"on Cohen's account\" sits between the subject and the verb but does not change the subject's number. Standard English requires the verb to agree with the actual head noun of the subject phrase, so the singular \"response\" takes the singular \"offers.\" The simple present tense fits the surrounding sentence, which is reporting Cohen's general analytic claim rather than a one-time past action.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A (\"offer\"): surface match — \"offer\" is the plural present-tense form, which would agree with the closest-to-the-verb plural noun, \"organizations.\" This is the classic proximity-agreement error: the eye picks up the nearer noun and treats it as the subject, even though \"organizations\" is inside a prepositional phrase modifying \"response\" and cannot govern the verb.\n- Choice C (\"have offered\"): scope shift — \"have offered\" is plural and present perfect, which links a prior action to a present reference point. Even setting aside the number error, the surrounding sentence is stating Cohen's general analytic position about a class of cases, not narrating a sequence of events that began in the past and reaches the present, so present perfect aspect does not fit the sentence's framing.\n- Choice D (\"are offering\"): inverse — \"are offering\" is plural and present progressive, which describes an action in progress at the moment of utterance. The sentence is reporting a settled position in Cohen's argument, not a process unfolding right now; progressive aspect treats a static analytic claim as if it were an ongoing event, which clashes with the sentence's framing.",
