          "explanation": "The correct answer is A, \"theorists had not anticipated:\". The material before the blank is a complete independent clause: \"the Berkeley anthropologist Nancy Scheper-Hughes argued that high infant mortality in a Brazilian shantytown produced something earlier theorists had not anticipated.\" The material after the blank — \"a culturally patterned restraint of attachment that mothers learned in conditions of chronic deprivation, rather than the universal grief reaction that mid-twentieth-century theory had assumed\" — is an extended noun phrase that names and unpacks what that unanticipated thing actually was. Standard English uses a colon to attach an explanatory or amplifying noun phrase to a complete preceding sentence, and \"earlier theorists had not anticipated\" sits inside the preceding clause as an integrated relative phrase that needs no internal punctuation. Choice A leaves the relative phrase punctuation-free and then introduces the amplifying noun phrase with a colon.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B (\"theorists had not anticipated\"): inverse — supplying no boundary punctuation at all between the independent clause and the long appositive noun phrase that follows leaves the reader without a signal that an amplifying definition has begun. The two stretches collapse into a single run-on string; the reader cannot tell whether \"a culturally patterned restraint of attachment\" is a continuation of \"had not anticipated\" or a separate gloss on what the clause has just named.\n- Choice C (\"theorists, had not anticipated:\"): scope shift — this option places a comma between the subject of the relative phrase (\"earlier theorists\") and its verb (\"had not anticipated\"), splitting a tightly bound subject-verb pair. Standard English does not place a comma between a subject and its verb, even when the subject phrase is multi-word; the comma here is misread as paired punctuation around \"theorists,\" but no second comma appears, so the construction signals nothing it can complete.\n- Choice D (\"theorists; had not anticipated:\"): surface match — a semicolon is the standard mark for joining two independent clauses, and the layout of this option resembles such a join. But \"had not anticipated:\" is not a complete clause; it is the predicate half of the relative phrase \"earlier theorists had not anticipated,\" which has been wrongly cut between its subject and verb. A semicolon between a noun and its verb is ungrammatical regardless of what follows."
