          "correctAnswer": "A",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is A, \"follow; however,\". The material before the conjunctive adverb is a complete independent clause: \"once the documentary record had been settled, an official memorial could follow.\" The material that follows \"however\" is also a complete independent clause: \"Ari Kelman shows, in *A Misplaced Massacre* (2013), that the establishment of a national historic site stalled for more than a century because federal agencies, the U.S. military, and Cheyenne and Arapaho descendant communities held incompatible understandings of what had happened at the site and what its memory should mean.\" Standard English requires a semicolon (or a period) before a conjunctive adverb that joins two independent clauses, and a comma after the adverb to set it off as a sentence-level interrupter. Choice A places the semicolon before \"however\" and the comma after, the canonical CB form.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B (\"follow, however;\"): inverse — B reverses the punctuation around \"however,\" placing a comma before and a semicolon after. That ordering treats \"however\" as the closing word of the first clause rather than as an interrupter at the start of the second. The semicolon then falls inside the second clause (between \"however\" and \"Ari Kelman shows\"), which is not where a clause boundary actually sits, and the comma before \"however\" produces a comma splice between the first independent clause and the conjunctive adverb that should bridge to the second.\n- Choice C (\"follow, however,\"): surface match — C surrounds \"however\" with paired commas, the punctuation Standard English uses when \"however\" sits inside a single independent clause as a parenthetical interrupter (as in \"the official memorial, however, did not follow\"). The pattern looks tidy and balanced and produces a familiar-looking sentence shape, but here \"however\" sits between two complete independent clauses rather than inside one. A pair of commas across that boundary leaves the two clauses joined by nothing stronger than a comma, which is a comma splice.\n- Choice D (\"follow however,\"): scope shift — a different SEC error. D supplies no punctuation before \"however\" and places only a comma after it. Without any mark before \"however,\" the first independent clause runs straight into the conjunctive adverb, fusing the two clauses into a run-on. The comma after \"however\" then sets off the adverb on one side only, an unmatched bracketing that leaves the boundary between the two clauses unmarked."
